\section{Choosing a view}{

For \code{plot()}:

Marginal fit and pairwise plots require strict marginal diagnostics from
\code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} with \code{diagnostic_mode = "both"} or \code{"marginal_fit"} for
supported MML RSM/PCM models. Positive residuals indicate more observed
category usage or agreement than expected; negative values indicate less.
Returned \code{full_table} and \code{retention} distinguish omitted and unavailable
rows. A flag is a descriptive review rule, not a calibrated test.

Bubble plots place measures against mean-square fit, or Infit against Outfit.
Bubble size can represent SE or observation count; neither a review band
nor bubble size alone justifies deleting an element.

The PCA convenience route can derive PCA from the diagnostic result.
For repeated PCA displays, first save \code{\link[=analyze_residual_pca]{analyze_residual_pca()}} and use its
\code{plot()} method. Parallel-reference generation remains an analysis step.
}

\section{Expected point-measure correlation under ordinary JML}{

For ordinary additive RSM/PCM JML without group-mean anchors,
\code{measures$PTMEAExpected} adds a conditional reference beside observed
\code{PTMEA}. Let \code{x} be the oriented generating measure, \code{e} the expected score,
\code{v} its conditional model variance, and \code{N} the sum of frequency weights
within the element. The reference is
\deqn{\frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,e)}{
\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}_w(x)\{\operatorname{Var}_w(e)+(N-2)\bar v_w/N\}}}.}
Weighted moments use the same observations and normalized frequency weights.
\code{PTMEAExpectedAsymptotic} omits the \code{(N-2)/N} adjustment. The correction is
Linacre's published finite-count reporting approximation; it is not an exact
expectation of the sample correlation, a significance test or a confidence
interval. Calibration is held fixed. Different administered PCM scales are
retained when calculating each observation's expectation and variance.

\code{PTMEAExpectedN}, \code{PTMEAExpectedFactor}, \code{PTMEAExpectedStatus} and
\code{PTMEAExpectedReason} identify the calculation and unavailable states.
At \code{N = 2} the variance term vanishes; this does not establish perfect
measurement or fit. Below \code{N = 2}, the corrected reference is unavailable
(FACETS 4.5.0 can instead display a boundary value). The asymptotic reference
remains available if its moments exist. Arbitrarily normalizing weights
changes the corrected reference: use replication semantics for comparison.
Constant predictors, invalid moments, or observations involving unanchored
extreme main effects withhold the complete element's expected reference;
they are not silently dropped. Fixed finite anchors remain usable.

FACETS 4.5.0 fixed-calibration PCM checks cover missing assignments and
unit, integer, fractional and unequal replication weights. These checks
establish a reporting convention within that scope, not general diagnostic
calibration. Observed \code{PTMEA} retains its existing definition. MML, corrected
JML, GPCM, fitted interactions and group-mean anchors do not receive the new
expected-correlation columns. Recreate older diagnostics from the saved fit
to obtain them; old saved results are not relabeled or silently recalculated.
See Linacre (2008), \emph{The Expected Value of a Point-Biserial (or Similar)
Correlation}, Rasch Measurement Transactions, 22:1, 1154, including its later
finite-count note: \url{https://www.rasch.org/rmt/rmt221e.htm}.
}

